(function(){"use strict";const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const revisions={"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-33":{"number":33,"title":"The Unfinished Song","word_count":1226,"content":"Greg had written four lines and crossed out three.\n\nHe showed me the page without pretending it was finished.\n\n\"Something's missing,\" he said.\n\nThe Starwake Pier stage was empty that morning. The western boards had reopened after inspection, but the public performance area wasn't booked until the weekend. A breeze rattled the spare chairs in the storage shed. Greg had brought his repaired guitar; the scratch along the body still caught the light.\n\nMaui followed us down the pier carrying a basket of breakfast rolls.\n\n\"Great!\" he said when Greg explained the problem. \"I'll give you a verse about the sea. Storms, great deeds, an enormous hero—\"\n\n\"I was hoping for something smaller,\" Greg said.\n\n\"Small hero?\"\n\n\"No hero, actually.\"\n\nMaui looked personally wounded.\n\nGreg played the four-note phrase again, the one he'd built from the ferry bell at Tideglass. The fifth note rose and settled in a way that reminded me of somebody returning after a long absence.\n\n\"See?\" he said. \"After that it needs a breath, but whenever I write words, I fill it up.\"\n\nMaui leaned on the railing. \"The problem is you're thinking too quietly. The ocean—\"\n\n\"Can you just sit with me for a while?\" Greg interrupted.\n\nThere was nothing angry in his voice. He looked tired.\n\nMaui stared. \"You don't need me to do anything?\"\n\n\"I asked because I like having you around. You're good at looking at the water without getting bored.\"\n\n\"I'm also good at a lot of other things.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nThe silence that followed made me want to jump in and explain both men to each other. Maui was trying to contribute; Greg wanted company. They could probably solve it themselves.\n\nSo I carried the breakfast basket to the bench and passed out the rolls.\n\nGreg played again.\n\nMaui lasted almost two minutes without speaking. Then he pointed toward the far end of the pier.\n\n\"That board is loose.\"\n\n\"It passed inspection,\" I said.\n\n\"It's making a sound.\"\n\nGreg stopped playing. \"What kind of sound?\"\n\nMaui tapped the railing once, listening. \"Like the line on a boat when it starts taking weight. Not danger exactly. Just something asking to be checked.\"\n\nGreg looked at his guitar. \"Huh.\"\n\nI knew the look. He'd found a thought he wanted to keep.\n\n\"Don't make him sing about the board,\" I warned.\n\nGreg grinned. \"I might.\"\n\nMaui looked pleased. \"Finally. A subject worthy of a song.\"\n\nThe three of us sat until the wind shifted. We listened to ropes clicking against cleats and water moving below the supports. The low note from the old drainage passage did not sound; Fen had recorded an ordinary tide that morning. There was no mystery demanding we stop enjoying breakfast.\n\nAfter a while Greg asked Maui, \"What's the sound you miss most from home?\"\n\nMaui didn't answer immediately.\n\nThen he said, \"The moment just before a wave breaks on the reef. People hear the crash. I hear the water pulling itself together.\"\n\nGreg nodded and played a note without words.\n\nMaui listened.\n\n\"That one,\" he said quietly.\n\nGreg tried it again. He moved two fingers along the strings, leaving a longer gap after the fifth note.\n\nI could see Maui wanting to fill it. He opened his mouth, closed it, and looked down at his hands.\n\n\"Do you want me to do something?\" he finally asked.\n\n\"Stay,\" Greg said.\n\nMaui nodded.\n\nThe answer was almost painfully simple.\n\nBefore Steven came, Tamsin walked down the safe side of the pier with a clipboard tucked beneath her arm. She heard the same faint rattle Maui had pointed out and stopped.\n\n\"Good ear,\" she told him. \"It passed the earlier check, but this wind is different. I'll log it before the next set.\"\n\nMaui looked so pleased that Greg grinned.\n\n\"See? You helped without writing me a sea-king anthem.\"\n\n\"I still have excellent rhymes available.\"\n\n\"Keep them for later.\"\n\nTamsin tested the cleat and marked the board for a second inspection before the weekend performance. It wasn't an emergency, and she didn't need the whole stage closed. The tiny distinction mattered: noticing a problem wasn't the same as declaring everything ruined, and somebody qualified had to decide what the sound meant.\n\nGreg stopped playing until Tamsin finished. He asked whether the inspection would change his rehearsal time and offered to move to the sheltered east bench if it did. She said that would be sensible, thanked him, and went back to her work.\n\nWe carried the guitar case to the bench. Maui looked at the cleared stage.\n\n\"I thought the sound was only something to fix.\"\n\n\"Sometimes it's a piece of a song,\" Greg said. \"Sometimes it's a safety problem. You can listen for both.\"\n\nMaui considered that, then leaned against the railing with his breakfast roll.\n\nI wanted to say something important about the way he was learning. Instead I asked if he wanted the last piece of bread.\n\n\"Obviously.\"\n\nGreg held out his hand for it first. Maui stared at him in betrayal. They argued cheerfully over the roll until I tore it in half, which struck me as considerably better music than a speech about sharing.\n\nAround midday Steven arrived carrying a small tin of snacks. He waved to me, hugged Greg, and asked whether he was interrupting.\n\n\"Not at all,\" Greg said. \"We're doing complicated artistic work.\"\n\nSteven looked at the three of us sitting silently.\n\n\"Looks intense.\"\n\n\"Very,\" Maui agreed.\n\nSteven laughed and settled at the edge of the bench. He didn't ask to hear a private song before it was ready. Greg played a bit of the new melody because he wanted to, not because his son had to be an audience.\n\nBy late afternoon the song still had only two lines.\n\nGreg looked happier.\n\nOn the walk back to Tideglass, Maui carried the empty basket. He had a strange expression, as though he'd done a difficult task and wasn't sure whether anyone had noticed.\n\n\"Did you enjoy today?\" I asked.\n\n\"I was waiting for Greg to need me.\"\n\n\"He did.\"\n\n\"I didn't fix anything.\"\n\n\"You listened.\"\n\n\"Very heroic.\"\n\n\"Not everything is supposed to be.\"\n\nHe stopped beside the sea-glass garden. The raised beds were full of the tiny, surviving signs of the season. A white flower bent in the salt wind but did not break.\n\n\"Sometimes I worry,\" he said, \"that if I'm not doing something remarkable, you won't need me.\"\n\nI took his hand. \"I don't want you because you're necessary. I want you because I like the person who sits beside me, even when there's nothing to rescue.\"\n\nHe looked down at me.\n\n\"That person says very few intelligent things.\"\n\n\"True.\"\n\n\"Terrible cook.\"\n\n\"Frequently.\"\n\n\"Excellent at carrying blankets.\"\n\n\"Legendary.\"\n\nHe laughed and drew me close enough to kiss. The kiss was gentle, a little shy, warmed by the long ordinary day.\n\nLater Greg sent a note. He had kept the space between the notes, written one new line, and left the song unfinished.\n\nBeneath it he added: *Tell Maui thanks for sitting with me.*\n\nI showed Maui.\n\nHe read it twice.\n\nThen he put the note beside the tiny wooden fish, where he kept things that made him remember the difference between being admired and being wanted.\n\nThat night he didn't tell me a heroic story.\n\nWe listened to the sea until we were ready to go inside."},"maui-34":{"number":34,"title":"A Nail from the Commonwealth","word_count":1238,"content":"The new fastener looked perfect until I pulled it from the board.\n\nOrange rust coated the threads. The metal was rough where it should have been smooth, and the hole in the timber had widened enough for seawater to gather around it.\n\nMaui stared at it.\n\n\"That came from Danse.\"\n\n\"From Brasswatch,\" I corrected. \"It might have come through his workshop. That doesn't make it his fault.\"\n\n\"It was supposed to last.\"\n\n\"So was the old rope.\"\n\nFen examined the piece under a magnifying lens. We'd found it during the morning inspection at the lower landing, where Lio's skiff had begun knocking against a repair bracket. Nobody had been injured. We had taken the mooring out of service immediately.\n\nTamsin joined us with the purchase record.\n\n\"Salvage fastener,\" she read. \"Recovered from a dry storage shed in the Commonwealth. Inspected for shape and load.\"\n\n\"Not for salt,\" Fen said.\n\nTamsin closed her eyes.\n\n\"Not for salt.\"\n\nMaui folded his arms. \"Why didn't anyone check?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe winced. \"Right. That's the question. Not the accusation.\"\n\nWe carried the rusted nail to Brasswatch.\n\nDanse was teaching Renn how to test a water pump. He took one look at the fastener and put the training piece aside.\n\n\"That's not acceptable,\" he said.\n\n\"Can you tell us why?\" I asked.\n\nHe turned the nail under the light.\n\n\"Corrosion. The protective coating was already compromised. Dry use might not have exposed it quickly, but coastal spray would.\"\n\nToph arrived from the next workroom, following the sound of our conversation.\n\n\"Something's wrong with the dock?\"\n\n\"One bracket,\" Tamsin said. \"The rest is being inspected.\"\n\nToph held the fastener carefully, testing its weight. \"This isn't magic, is it?\"\n\nDanse looked offended. \"No.\"\n\n\"Good. I hate when people make bad engineering interesting by calling it a curse.\"\n\nRenn fetched the batch ledger. There were twelve nails from the same salvaged lot. Five had been used at Tideglass, three on a protected shed in Brasswatch, and four remained in storage.\n\n\"We need all of them identified,\" Danse said.\n\nMaui looked toward the door. \"I'll go tear them out.\"\n\n\"Not all at once,\" Tamsin said. \"Some are holding things in place until we brace the structure.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nWe made a list of locations and assigned inspection teams. Fen closed the affected landing section. Tamsin designed temporary supports. Danse checked the material specification and compared replacement options. Renn contacted the supplier without accusing them of fraud; the material had been sold for general salvage, not coastal structural service.\n\nThe supplier replied that afternoon. They had no evidence the pieces had been rated for salt exposure.\n\nIt was a mistake, not a conspiracy.\n\nI could see Maui struggling with that.\n\n\"A villain would be easier,\" he admitted while we waited for the new hardware. \"You fight them. You win. Everyone gets a safe dock.\"\n\n\"Instead we have paperwork.\"\n\n\"An evil thing.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nRenn had another question before we ordered replacements.\n\n\"How do we know the next lot is actually what the label claims?\"\n\nIt was the sort of question I was embarrassed not to have asked. Danse put down the faulty fastener and looked at the supplier's old order sheet.\n\n\"We should have checked the material specification against the conditions at Tideglass,\" he admitted. \"A second invoice isn't a test.\"\n\nTamsin suggested separating the remaining salvaged stock into marked bins: dry interior use, pending verification, and unsafe. Fen wanted the rejected lot traced to its origin without claiming a faulty batch proved malicious intent. Lio wanted to know whether the temporary closure would cost the small boat crews an entire day of work.\n\n\"Not if we schedule the repairs around the safe tide window,\" Fen said. \"But they'll need notice.\"\n\nI wrote the closure times on a draft sign. Edda, who had come to deliver a route report, looked at it and pointed out that the detour described a set of steps without naming an accessible alternative. I added the upper path and asked her to test the directions on the ground before we posted them.\n\nMaui watched the growing list.\n\n\"Is there anything about this nail that doesn't involve a committee?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" Danse said. \"It fails in salt water.\"\n\nToph barked a laugh. So did I.\n\nThe new order listed the actual corrosion rating, the use conditions, and a person responsible for checking the certificate before installation. We set aside money for labor rather than asking the repair crew to donate its afternoon. I wanted the change to look more impressive than a receipt. It didn't.\n\nIt would, however, keep the next person from having to pull rust out of the same board while pretending nobody could have known.\n\nThe replacement fasteners cost more. They had a suitable corrosion rating and came with a maintenance schedule. Mara agreed to cover part of the immediate cost from the repair fund; the remaining expense went to the next council meeting instead of disappearing into someone's unpaid time.\n\nWe worked in daylight. Fen supervised the lift. Lio held a support while the first bracket came free. Maui braced the timber without taking over the job. Danse and Tamsin checked the fit. Toph tested the movement through the stone foundation and reported what she could feel, without pretending she could inspect the metal's internal condition.\n\nWhen the first replacement passed, we cheered.\n\nThen we tested four more.\n\nBy sunset the mooring was usable again at a reduced load. The final load test would happen after the next tide cycle. We posted the restriction at the landing in large letters.\n\nMaui looked at the sign.\n\n\"Does everything here come with conditions?\"\n\n\"Anything worth keeping does,\" Fen said.\n\nI thought about the relationship agreement on our kitchen table. That had conditions too: honesty, choice, the ability to revise. It hadn't made our affection less real.\n\nDanse washed his hands at the work sink.\n\n\"I authorized the first batch for use,\" he said. \"I'll document where I failed to check the environment.\"\n\nTamsin looked at him.\n\n\"I approved the installation.\"\n\n\"And I carried the materials,\" Maui added.\n\nFen nodded. \"Then the report will show what each of you did. Nobody benefits if you turn the record into a competition for blame.\"\n\nRenn wrote the final note. Their handwriting was small and tidy.\n\nAt dinner, the group ate fish soup by the sheltered garden. Danse stayed for one bowl before returning to Brasswatch. Iroh sent tea from Jasmine Steps. Greg arrived late and asked whether the dock was going to make a dramatic noise again.\n\n\"Not if we can help it,\" I said.\n\nHe looked faintly disappointed.\n\nWhen everyone left, Maui held the corroded nail in his palm.\n\n\"We should keep it,\" he said.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"So I remember to ask what a thing was made for, not just whether I can lift it.\"\n\nWe set it on the shelf beneath the tide chart, in a little clear jar labeled *Not for the sea.*\n\nMaui leaned toward me.\n\n\"Can I kiss you before you make another label?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe kissed me until I forgot what I'd been about to write. Later I found the pencil under the table and laughed.\n\nThe next morning Fen's report came back: the tide test had passed within the posted limits.\n\nThe jar remained where we could see it.\n\nNot every failure deserved a legend. Some deserved the right replacement part and the humility to read its specification."},"maui-37":{"number":37,"title":"The Tide Between Us","word_count":1230,"content":"Vel was sitting at our kitchen table when I came home.\n\nI recognized the small wooden fish beside her cup. It was the one she'd returned after Maui refused to shame Tomas for leaving Lanternweald. I hadn't seen her since.\n\nMaui stood at the stove looking uncomfortable.\n\n\"Jasper,\" he said, \"Vel needs a place to stay until the north road clears.\"\n\nI looked from him to her.\n\n\"Does she?\"\n\nVel lifted her chin. \"My cart axle broke. The shelter is full.\"\n\n\"The shelter isn't full,\" I said, because I'd helped with the count that afternoon. \"There are rooms available on the upper level.\"\n\nMaui shot me a warning glance.\n\nVel looked wounded.\n\nI put down my bag.\n\n\"Can we talk outside?\" I asked Maui.\n\nHe followed me to the porch. The sea wind was strong enough to make the lantern shiver on its hook.\n\n\"You brought someone into our home without asking,\" I said.\n\n\"She needed help.\"\n\n\"Yes. She deserves help. That's not the same as deciding she can sleep here.\"\n\n\"She's had a hard day.\"\n\n\"So have I.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"You're being harsh.\"\n\nI felt something hot and unpleasant rise behind my ribs.\n\n\"And you're being generous with space that isn't only yours.\"\n\nHis face changed. We stood very still.\n\nThen the door opened and Vel stepped onto the porch.\n\n\"I can hear you,\" she said.\n\nI closed my eyes.\n\n\"Vel, I'm sorry. You shouldn't have had to listen to us argue over your head.\"\n\nShe looked from me to Maui.\n\n\"I thought he said I could stay.\"\n\n\"He did,\" Maui answered quietly. \"Without checking with Jasper first. That's my mistake.\"\n\nVel's mouth tightened.\n\n\"Then I'll go.\"\n\n\"Not into the rain,\" I said. \"There's a room at the hall. I can walk you there and help with the cart arrangements tomorrow, if you'd like.\"\n\nShe stared at me.\n\n\"I don't want charity.\"\n\n\"Then we can talk about the actual cost of the room. The hall has a posted rate for people who can pay, and emergency funds for people who can't. You decide what you want to share.\"\n\nVel looked away.\n\nAfter a moment she nodded.\n\nMaui fetched her bag. He offered to escort her, but she asked me instead. The choice seemed to hurt him; he didn't argue.\n\nWe walked to the hall along the safe ridge. Vel spoke only once.\n\n\"I thought if I came back, he'd forgive me for being angry.\"\n\n\"He might,\" I said. \"But you don't have to stay in his house to talk to him.\"\n\nShe looked at me sharply.\n\n\"I didn't want to make trouble.\"\n\n\"I believe you.\"\n\nWe checked the room. It was clean, dry, and had a working lamp. A volunteer explained how the door locked from the inside. Vel accepted the key and asked whether Mara would be selling bread in the morning.\n\n\"She will.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nWhen I returned home, Maui was washing the cups. He'd scrubbed the table, too, as though the wood itself had taken part in the argument.\n\n\"She's safe?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI took off my coat.\n\n\"I don't want to fight,\" I said.\n\n\"Neither do I.\"\n\n\"Then we have to talk about what happened.\"\n\nHe set down the cloth.\n\n\"I wanted to prove I could still help her. After Tomas left, she stopped believing in me. I thought if I gave her a place to stay, she might understand.\"\n\n\"That's not your house to give alone.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nI sat across from him.\n\n\"Your kindness became an unasked favor from me. And when I objected, you made me sound cruel.\"\n\nHe looked stricken.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\nI held up a hand. \"I wasn't fair either. I corrected Vel in front of you both instead of asking what she needed. I treated her like a problem before I'd even listened.\"\n\nMaui exhaled.\n\n\"We were trying to be good people.\"\n\n\"We were trying to look like good people,\" I said. \"At least a little.\"\n\nHe gave a rueful laugh.\n\nI thought of Vel's expression when she realized Maui's invitation hadn't included me. She had come in from bad weather, tired and angry, and had been made to feel like a problem two people were arguing about.\n\n\"There's something else,\" I said.\n\nMaui waited.\n\n\"The hall isn't a punishment. And we should stop talking as if shelter is a prize that goes to people who make us feel like heroes.\"\n\nHe rubbed his face. \"I know. I panicked when she said she needed somewhere safe.\"\n\n\"Then tomorrow, ask the hall coordinator what the actual emergency-housing procedure is. Don't ask for Vel's private notes. Ask what we can do when someone comes to our door.\"\n\nHe nodded slowly.\n\n\"We could keep a list of places with space and safe routes,\" he said.\n\n\"With the coordinator's permission. And it needs to be kept current.\"\n\nMaui found the notebook beneath the calendar and wrote *Ask first. Check capacity. Don't promise another person's room.* His handwriting took up almost an entire page.\n\n\"You're making a law for our kitchen,\" he observed.\n\n\"Not a law. A boundary we can both understand when we're frightened.\"\n\n\"What if you aren't home?\"\n\n\"Help with an immediate safety need. Give someone warmth, water, a way to contact a registrar. But don't assign my bed or somebody else's privacy because you want to feel useful.\"\n\nHe looked at the empty chair where Vel had sat.\n\n\"I didn't let her choose either,\" he said.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"I'll apologize to her for that. If she wants to hear it.\"\n\nWe sat together until the kettle stopped hissing. Then he wrote a second line in smaller letters: *An apology isn't a ticket back inside.* I didn't tell him how much it mattered that he'd thought of that himself.\n\nThat was the beginning of the repair, not the end.\n\nWe talked about shared space. We agreed that either of us could offer immediate help in a true emergency, but an overnight guest required discussion when both of us were available. If someone needed shelter and our answer was no, we would help them find an alternative rather than leave them without options.\n\nMaui wrote it beneath our calendar agreement, in the same enormous handwriting.\n\nThen he stared at the page.\n\n\"Do you still want to live with me?\"\n\nI looked at the sink full of cups and the crooked fish drawing beside the stove.\n\n\"Yes,\" I said. \"But wanting doesn't remove the work.\"\n\nHe came around the table, slowly enough for me to choose whether to meet him.\n\n\"Can I hold you?\"\n\nI stood.\n\nHe wrapped his arms around me. His breath shook once against my hair.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" he whispered.\n\n\"So am I.\"\n\nI lifted my face. His kiss was gentle, then warmer when I drew him closer. There were no fireworks, no perfect words, and no audience. Later we closed the door and left the rest of the night between us.\n\nIn the morning, Maui went to the hall.\n\nHe asked Vel whether she wanted to talk. She told him not yet.\n\nHe accepted it.\n\nHe returned with bread from Mara, a message that Vel's cart would be repaired after the morning inspection, and the uncomfortable realization that apology didn't entitle him to immediate forgiveness.\n\nWe ate breakfast together.\n\nThe tide was coming in.\n\nFor once, neither of us mistook that movement for a reason to hurry."},"maui-45":{"number":45,"title":"The Last Big Performance","word_count":1259,"content":"The invitation was written in letters three inches high.\n\nMAUI, HERO OF THE TIDE, WILL OPEN THE REPAIR CELEBRATION.\n\nHe stared at the poster with an expression I couldn't read.\n\n\"Well,\" I said.\n\n\"Well.\"\n\nI waited for him to lift his chin and begin planning a speech about himself.\n\nInstead he asked, \"Who decided that?\"\n\nPip appeared from behind the lantern table.\n\n\"The entertainment committee. They thought people would like something exciting after all the repairs.\"\n\nMaui looked around the square.\n\nThe temporary surface channel had passed its second inspection. Riverstone's bridge was almost ready for its final load test. The western Starwake boards had reopened, and the Brasswatch generator had stopped behaving like an offended dragon. The village had survived a string of storms and repairs, but the lower service passage remained closed while engineers studied its age and the old decisions surrounding it.\n\nThere was reason to celebrate.\n\nThere was also a lot of work that didn't belong to one famous man.\n\n\"I don't want to be the opening act,\" Maui said.\n\nPip blinked.\n\n\"You don't?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nI didn't answer for him.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said.\n\nThe disappointment was real. I could see it cross Pip's face. They had hoped for a draw, something that would make the square full and the donation box heavier.\n\nMaui noticed.\n\n\"I can help with something else,\" he offered. \"If you need it.\"\n\nPip looked toward the pile of lantern hooks.\n\n\"Actually...\"\n\nWe spent the next two days preparing the celebration.\n\nGreg would play a short original set, because he had chosen to and could leave when it was over. The children from Tidewake wanted to perform their little story about Fen falling into the harbor. Fen protested until they discovered they had been given the role of the wise crab.\n\nIroh offered tea. Mara brought bread. Tamsin made sure the decorations were mounted on independent supports rather than the ancient arches. Edda checked the viewing areas so people could sit or stand without blocking the routes.\n\nMaui helped Pip sort hooks.\n\nIt was possibly the least glamorous work he'd ever done.\n\nHe was very bad at sorting.\n\n\"Those are the long ones,\" Pip told him.\n\n\"They look short.\"\n\n\"Compared with your hand, everything looks short.\"\n\nI laughed from the next table.\n\nMaui held up a hook.\n\n\"This one?\"\n\n\"Medium.\"\n\nHe sighed.\n\n\"How can there be so many kinds of hook?\"\n\n\"How can there be so many kinds of fish?\" Pip asked.\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Excellent point.\"\n\nOn the evening before the festival, the volunteers met to confirm what the celebration actually required. Pip had written a cheerful program, but the schedule had no break between the children's kite display and the lantern procession. Greg was listed for three songs despite having agreed to two.\n\n\"He'll still do three,\" somebody said. \"He likes playing.\"\n\n\"Ask him,\" I said.\n\nWe found Greg sorting cables by the stage. He was happy to play two. The third song wasn't ready, and he'd promised to be back in Beach City the next morning. Nobody was entitled to turn a friendly offer into an obligation.\n\nPip revised the program without grumbling. The children chose a shorter kite demonstration because Fen had posted an afternoon wind warning. Tamsin checked that the lantern stands could be moved without blocking Edda's turning space. Mara counted the bread orders against the bakery's actual flour supply.\n\nMaui carried chairs while we worked. When one volunteer asked whether he could still give a short heroic introduction, he stopped and looked at the children waiting to test their lantern hooks.\n\n\"I'd rather introduce the people who did the repairs,\" he said. \"If they want to speak.\"\n\nSeveral of them didn't. The council decided a name could go on the public thanks list only with permission; private volunteers could be thanked collectively. One worker asked not to be identified at all. Pip crossed the name out without asking why.\n\nLater Maui admitted that he'd imagined the crowd chanting for him.\n\n\"You can want applause,\" I said. \"You just can't collect it on someone else's behalf.\"\n\n\"I know. I wish knowing didn't make me feel ridiculous.\"\n\n\"It doesn't. You look ridiculous when you try to hide behind a basket of lantern hooks.\"\n\nHe peered over the basket. \"Then my disguise has failed.\"\n\nI laughed and kissed him on the cheek. There was no grand lesson written on the program, only accurate times, safe paths, and people who'd agreed to be there.\n\nBy festival morning, the poster had been replaced. It listed the schedule without turning any person into the center of the world. Maui's name appeared under *volunteer setup*, alongside Pip, Lio, Edda, and a dozen others.\n\nHe looked at it for a long moment.\n\n\"Do you regret it?\" I asked.\n\n\"Some of it.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\n\"I like being seen.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I think I always will.\"\n\n\"That's not a crime.\"\n\nHe looked relieved.\n\n\"I just don't want to need it to know who I am.\"\n\nWe stood together beneath the lanterns.\n\nThen Lio shouted for help with the stage ramp, and Maui went to ask what they needed.\n\nThe celebration began late because the first tray of bread had to be rescued from an overenthusiastic oven. Nobody minded. Aang helped the children launch a few kites from the safe ridge, keeping sacred Air Nomad traditions separate from a local craft festival. Toph called the whole event \"terribly sentimental\" and then refused to leave before the last performance.\n\nGreg played a song about a ferry bell.\n\nIt was quieter than the crowd expected.\n\nPeople listened.\n\nI saw Maui standing at the edge of the square, holding a spare lantern pole in one hand. He wasn't watching the stage. He was watching a child who couldn't quite reach the hook they'd been assigned.\n\nHe knelt, held the pole steady, and asked whether they wanted help.\n\nThe child nodded.\n\nTogether they hung the lantern.\n\nNo applause.\n\nNo one even turned to look.\n\nSomething in my chest ached.\n\nLater, when the crowd began moving toward the food stalls, Maui came to find me.\n\n\"Did I miss anything?\"\n\n\"The wise crab was excellent.\"\n\n\"Naturally.\"\n\n\"Greg played beautifully.\"\n\n\"I heard.\"\n\n\"And you helped Pip.\"\n\nHe shrugged.\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n\"I saw you with the lantern.\"\n\nHis eyes widened.\n\n\"You did?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked almost shy.\n\n\"Was it all right?\"\n\n\"It was lovely.\"\n\nHe let out a breath.\n\nI took his hand and pulled him behind the sheltered garden wall, out of the flow of people. The lanterns made soft gold patterns on the stone.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\" I asked.\n\nHis smile was sudden and warm.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nI kissed him. His hands settled carefully at my waist, and for a few moments the festival noise became a distant, pleasant blur. We laughed against each other when a paper fish caught in his hair.\n\n\"Your audience has arrived,\" I whispered.\n\n\"A terrible critic.\"\n\n\"Very.\"\n\nWe rejoined the crowd after the lantern keeper checked the final hooks. The donation box held enough to fund part of the next inspection, and the public ledger recorded the amount. No one promised the service tunnel would reopen because the celebration had gone well.\n\nAt the end, Pip thanked the volunteers.\n\nMaui was named with everyone else.\n\nHe clapped for the children.\n\nWhen we walked home, he carried no trophy.\n\nOnly a small bent hook he'd rescued from the sorting pile, because he thought we could use it in the garden.\n\nWe couldn't.\n\nI let him keep it anyway."}},"after-always":{"after-always-31":{"number":31,"title":"A House That Still Stands","word_count":1421,"content":"We returned to Spinner's End in March.\n\nThe house had survived winter.\n\nSnape seemed offended by this.\n\n“It leans,” he said from the pavement.\n\n“It leaned before.”\n\n“The eastern gutter has separated.”\n\n“That is not structural collapse.”\n\n“It is an invitation.”\n\n“To rain?”\n\n“To rot.”\n\n“Very dramatic.”\n\n“Very accurate.”\n\nHe unlocked the gate.\n\nThis time, I entered without needing to be asked twice.\n\nThat mattered.\n\nSo did the fact that he had invited me here for no professional reason at all.\n\nThe audit was finished.\n\nThe hearing was over.\n\nOur relationship—still a word that made him look as if he had bitten tinfoil—had lasted two months without anyone dying, fleeing the country, or drafting a new statute.\n\nSpinner's End was the first place we had come simply because it belonged to him.\n\nOr had.\n\nThat was the question.\n\nInside, dust had reclaimed the furniture. The table no longer bore the word traitor. The sitting room still looked searched but not destroyed. Snape stood in the doorway with one hand in his coat pocket.\n\n“I am considering selling it.”\n\nI looked at the narrow room.\n\n“Do you want to?”\n\n“I dislike it.”\n\n“That wasn't the question.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\nI smiled.\n\n“I learned from the best.”\n\n“Debatable.”\n\nWe spent the morning opening windows.\n\nCold spring air moved through rooms that had been closed too long. I shook dust from curtains. Snape repaired the gutter from the upstairs window with a charm and then complained that Muggle architecture required magical intervention to become tolerable.\n\n“You could just admit you're fixing your house.”\n\n“I am preventing deterioration pending disposition.”\n\n“Romantic.”\n\n“Stop using that word incorrectly.”\n\nI found a box beneath the stairs.\n\nNot hidden.\n\nJust forgotten.\n\nInside were old school things.\n\nA Slytherin scarf.\n\nBroken quills.\n\nA chipped brass cauldron.\n\nSeveral books with Severus Snape written inside in a cramped adolescent hand.\n\nAnd a pair of boots far too small for the man standing behind me.\n\nSnape went silent.\n\nI looked at the boots.\n\nWorn leather. Soles repaired twice. One lace replaced with cord.\n\n“Yours?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“How old?”\n\n“Eleven. Perhaps twelve.”\n\nI touched one lightly.\n\n“You kept them.”\n\n“I forgot them.”\n\n“Same result.”\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\nI did not say anything about poverty or neglect.\n\nI did not need to narrate his childhood back to him.\n\nI put the boots beside the box.\n\n“Keep or discard?” I asked.\n\nHe looked surprised by the practical question.\n\n“Discard.”\n\nI put them in the discard pile.\n\nHe stared at them.\n\n“Keep,” he said.\n\nI moved them back.\n\nThen he swore quietly.\n\nI laughed.\n\n“This is why people hate clearing houses.”\n\n“It is irrational.”\n\n“Probably.”\n\n“They serve no purpose.”\n\n“Neither does that terrible chair in your sitting room.”\n\n“The chair is functional.”\n\n“The chair is a threat.”\n\n“It has survived forty years.”\n\n“So has trauma. We don't praise everything for longevity.”\n\nHe gave me a look.\n\nI grinned.\n\nBy lunch, we had three piles: keep, discard, undecided.\n\nUndecided was largest.\n\nSnape stood over it in disgust.\n\n“This has accomplished nothing.”\n\n“You opened the windows.”\n\n“Heroic.”\n\n“You fixed the gutter.”\n\n“Necessary.”\n\n“You found your school scarf.”\n\n“Unfortunate.”\n\nI picked it up.\n\nGreen and silver, faded almost gray.\n\n“Put it down.”\n\nI held it against his chest.\n\nHe went rigid.\n\n“You looked adorable.”\n\n“I will end you.”\n\n“You were eleven.”\n\n“I was never adorable.”\n\n“That cannot possibly be true.”\n\nHe took the scarf from me and, after a pause, put it in the keep pile.\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Say nothing.”\n\n“I am saying nothing.”\n\n“Your face remains intolerable.”\n\nWe ate sandwiches in the kitchen.\n\nThe repair estimate arrived with a list of things a spell couldn't fix cheaply: a roofline that needed inspecting in ordinary weather, a cellar with poor airflow, and a fire exit that would be blocked if Severus filled the entire ground floor with laboratory furniture.\n\nHe read the paper as if he could intimidate it into better numbers.\n\n\"How much?\" I asked.\n\n\"More than the house is worth to a sensible buyer.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps we shouldn't pretend the house is a bargain.\"\n\nHe looked offended, though not at me this time.\n\nThe local builder had marked the unsafe part of the gutter and asked for permission before scheduling work. Severus had never met someone so unimpressed by the possibility of a perfectly executed repair charm. He seemed to find the experience both humiliating and oddly restful.\n\n\"I could do the visible work myself,\" he said.\n\n\"You could. But the builder needs to sign off on the structural work if you're going to have visitors or apprentices in here.\"\n\n\"I didn't say I wanted apprentices.\"\n\n\"Then don't invite any.\"\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nI suggested making two columns: what the house needed to stay safe for one person, and what it would need if he ever chose to make it a working space. Neither column was an argument that he had to keep it.\n\n\"I've told you before that I'm not asking for a claim on every part of your life,\" I said. \"We're ethically non-monogamous; I prefer polyamory. Loving me doesn't mean deciding where to live so you can prove the relationship is permanent.\"\n\n\"I was not planning to build you a shrine.\"\n\n\"Good. I would be terrible at dusting it.\"\n\nHe gave a reluctant laugh, then drew a line under the estimate. We could return to the question when he had numbers that were real rather than frightening. For today he paid the builder for the inspection. That felt like an answer without being a verdict.\n\nThe repaired table stood between us.\n\nSnape had brought food from Hogwarts because he did not trust anything left in the cupboards.\n\nI looked around.\n\n“What would you do with the house if you kept it?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“That seems wasteful.”\n\n“It is a house, not an assignment.”\n\n“Could be a laboratory.”\n\nHis eyes lifted.\n\nThe idea had occurred to him.\n\nInteresting.\n\n“A small one,” I continued. “Independent research. No students. No Board. No Ministry except when they inevitably annoy you.”\n\n“Ventilation is poor.”\n\n“So fix it.”\n\n“Plumbing is worse.”\n\n“Magic.”\n\n“The cellar floods.”\n\n“Drainage charm.”\n\n“You have an irritating answer for everything.”\n\n“You taught me.”\n\nHe looked away, but not before I saw interest.\n\n“Why here?” he asked.\n\nThat was the real question.\n\nWhy build anything new in a place that held so much old pain?\n\nI considered.\n\n“You don't have to.”\n\nHis shoulders eased.\n\n“But keeping it wouldn't mean the childhood wins,” I continued. “Selling it wouldn't mean you escaped. It's just property unless you decide otherwise.”\n\nSnape stared at the tabletop.\n\n“Just a house,” he said.\n\n“Potentially a judgmental one.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nWe spent the afternoon in the back room measuring shelves.\n\nNot because he had decided.\n\nHe repeatedly informed me he had not decided.\n\nThen he drew three laboratory layouts on scrap parchment.\n\nThe first used the existing cellar.\n\nThe second expanded the kitchen wall.\n\nThe third converted the sitting room into a library and built a separate workroom in the yard.\n\n“You haven't decided,” I said.\n\n“Correct.”\n\n“Very undecided drawings.”\n\n“They are feasibility studies.”\n\n“Of course.”\n\nHe handed me the third.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Your opinion.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“Mine?”\n\n“You have one. Constantly.”\n\n“Do you want it?”\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI studied the plan.\n\n“The separate workroom.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“More light. Better ventilation. You could keep the house as a house instead of turning every room into proof of productivity.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“That last statement was unnecessary.”\n\n“Still true.”\n\nHe took the plan back.\n\nThe late sun angled through the window.\n\nFor once, Spinner's End did not look gray.\n\nDust moved gold in the light.\n\nSnape stood beside the old desk where he had once watched for Lily.\n\nI wondered whether he was seeing the past.\n\nThen he looked at me.\n\n“Would you come here?” he asked.\n\nThe question sounded casual only if one knew nothing about him.\n\n“To the laboratory?”\n\n“To the house.”\n\nMy heart turned over.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“For work?”\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\n“And otherwise?”\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe answer was enough.\n\nSnape's hand touched my waist.\n\n“Still judgmental?” he asked.\n\n“The house?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Less so.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Better company.”\n\nHe kissed me in the room where he had once waited at the window for someone else.\n\nThat did not erase the boy.\n\nIt did not replace Lily.\n\nIt did not transform the house into a symbol of healing because life was rarely that courteous.\n\nIt was simply a new memory.\n\nThe house still stood.\n\nSo did he.\n\nFor the moment, that was enough."},"after-always-32":{"number":32,"title":"What Lily Was and Was Not","word_count":1415,"content":"The first time I asked Snape whether he had been in love with Lily or in love with what she represented, he nearly choked on tea.\n\nWe were at Spinner's End, sitting on the floor because the terrible chair had finally collapsed under a repair charm and Snape was taking this as a personal insult.\n\nHe set down his cup.\n\n“What an appalling question.”\n\n“Too personal?”\n\n“Too imprecise.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“That sounds promising.”\n\n“It should not.”\n\nI had not planned to ask.\n\nThe question had been circling since the hearing, sharpened by newspapers that kept reducing Lily to a symbol in Snape's story and Snape to a symbol in hers.\n\nI did not want to do the same.\n\n“You can refuse,” I said.\n\n“I am aware.”\n\nHe leaned back against the wall.\n\nThe sitting room smelled of dust and fresh plaster. One of his laboratory plans lay open on the floor between us.\n\n“At eleven,” he said, “I loved her because she was kind to me.”\n\nI stayed quiet.\n\n“At fifteen, I loved her because she was clever and stubborn and because she had become part of how I imagined my own future.”\n\nHis face tightened.\n\n“At sixteen, I loved her badly.”\n\nThe words were simple.\n\n“What does that mean?”\n\n“I wanted her understanding without giving sufficient respect to what she objected to. I resented people she cared for because I believed my history with her should matter more than her choices.”\n\nJames.\n\nHer friends.\n\nThe people Snape had hated or dismissed.\n\n“And later?”\n\n“Later I loved someone who was dead.”\n\nHis eyes moved toward the window.\n\n“That makes honesty difficult.”\n\n“Because she couldn't change?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe answer surprised me with its clarity.\n\n“Memory preserves what it preserves. Grief edits. Regret edits more.”\n\nI thought of the girl in the Pensieve. Laughing. Angry. Defending her sister. Refusing his apology when it was not enough.\n\n“She wasn't a saint,” I said.\n\nSnape's eyes flashed.\n\n“No.”\n\nThe force of agreement made me smile.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You sounded offended on her behalf.”\n\n“I am offended by most simplifications.”\n\n“Consistent.”\n\nHe looked down at his tea.\n\n“Lily could be impatient. Judgmental. Reckless when angry. She enjoyed being right more than she admitted.”\n\n“You liked that.”\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\n“You hated it when it was aimed at you.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Also consistent.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nI shifted closer, our shoulders touching against the wall.\n\n“Do you think she would forgive you?”\n\nSnape became very still.\n\nThe reporter had asked that.\n\nI regretted the question immediately.\n\n“Sorry.”\n\n“No.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe was not refusing.\n\nHe was answering.\n\n“No?”\n\n“I do not know,” he said. “And I no longer believe I am entitled to construct an answer for her.”\n\nThat felt important.\n\n“For years I imagined what she would say because there was no one to contradict me.”\n\nHis expression tightened.\n\n“That is not conversation. It is ventriloquism.”\n\nI let the words sit.\n\n“So you stopped?”\n\n“I am trying to.”\n\nI took his hand.\n\nHe looked at our fingers.\n\n“Does being with me feel disloyal to her?” I asked.\n\nHis head snapped toward me.\n\n“No.”\n\nImmediate.\n\nMy chest loosened.\n\n“Did it ever?”\n\nA pause.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI appreciated the truth more than a prettier answer.\n\n“When?”\n\n“The first time I wanted to kiss you.”\n\n“In Hogsmeade?”\n\n“Earlier.”\n\nI blinked.\n\n“How much earlier?”\n\nHe looked annoyed.\n\n“Why is that relevant?”\n\n“Severus.”\n\nThe name slipped out naturally.\n\nWe both froze.\n\nI had called him Snape almost from the beginning. He had called me whatever the moment allowed: Consultant, occasionally a ridiculous nickname based on some professional error, more often nothing at all because names were unnecessary in a room built for two.\n\nHis first name felt intimate in a way kissing had not.\n\n“Again,” he said quietly.\n\nMy pulse jumped.\n\n“Severus.”\n\nHis eyes closed for half a second.\n\nWhen they opened, he looked younger and older at once.\n\n“The first time,” he said, “was the night you caught the memory vial.”\n\nI stared.\n\n“That early?”\n\n“I did not say I wanted to act on it.”\n\n“You wanted to kiss me.”\n\n“I considered the possibility.”\n\n“That is Snape for wanted to kiss me.”\n\n“Do not become unbearable.”\n\n“Too late.”\n\nI was grinning.\n\nHe looked deeply irritated and slightly embarrassed.\n\nIt was wonderful.\n\nThen his expression sobered.\n\n“I felt disloyal because desire seemed like movement.”\n\n“Away from her?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And now?”\n\n“I understand movement is not replacement.”\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\n“Good.”\n\nHis gaze sharpened.\n\n“You were worried.”\n\n“Of course I was.”\n\n“You said neither of us was required to compete with the dead.”\n\n“I meant it. That doesn't mean I'm incapable of insecurity.”\n\nHe studied me.\n\n“What were you afraid of?”\n\nThat question was harder to answer.\n\nI looked at the cracked plaster on the opposite wall.\n\n“That I would become evidence that you were healed.”\n\nSnape's expression changed.\n\n“Explain.”\n\n“That people would look at us and say, see, he moved on, so everything is fine now. Or that you'd look at us that way.”\n\nHis face hardened with something like offense.\n\n“I would never use you that way.”\n\n“I know that now.”\n\n“And before?”\n\n“I didn't know anything before.”\n\nA fair point.\n\nHe looked down.\n\nA knock came from the front room. The builder had left a revised sketch under the door and gone, apparently aware that Severus would prefer not to be interrupted while deciding whether a chair could be accused of attempted murder.\n\nSeverus retrieved the sketch but did not open it. He stood with the paper in his hand.\n\n\"People have spent years trying to make Lily's death into a neat account of my life,\" he said. \"It was never neat.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You didn't know her.\"\n\n\"No. And that means there's a limit to what I can claim about her.\"\n\nHe considered that. I knew his history only through what he'd chosen to tell me and what had been recorded by others, some of it badly. It would have been easy to imagine that accepting his memories gave me ownership over her. It didn't.\n\n\"I don't need to be your only love,\" I said. \"You know I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. But that doesn't mean I have the right to sort your feelings into a system that makes sense to me. And I don't want you to use Lily as evidence that you're incapable of choosing a future.\"\n\nSeverus leaned against the doorway. \"You are remarkably determined to deprive me of a good excuse.\"\n\n\"Only the ones that make you miserable.\"\n\n\"That is a broad category.\"\n\nThe joke didn't solve anything. It gave us room to stay beside the difficulty without treating it as a test we had to pass before being affectionate. He opened the builder's sketch, frowned, and handed me the list of repairs that could wait.\n\nI took the pencil and wrote down a time for the next inspection. He didn't tell me the house was healed. He asked if I would like to come back with him, and I said yes, as long as we had time to do something besides repair walls.\n\n“You are not proof of recovery,” he said.\n\nThe sentence warmed me unexpectedly.\n\n“You are not a cure. You are not a reward. You are not a substitute.”\n\nEach word precise.\n\n“What am I?”\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\n“A person asking irritating questions on my sitting-room floor.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nThen he added, “Whom I love.”\n\nEverything stopped.\n\nNo build-up.\n\nNo speech.\n\nHe looked as startled as I felt.\n\n“Severus.”\n\n“Do not.”\n\n“You just—”\n\n“I am aware.”\n\n“You said—”\n\n“I am aware.”\n\nHis face had gone red.\n\nI was smiling so hard it hurt.\n\n“This is unbearable,” he muttered.\n\n“I love you too.”\n\nSilence.\n\nHis eyes snapped to mine.\n\nThe room changed.\n\nNot because love was new.\n\nBecause language had caught up.\n\nSnape looked down at our hands.\n\n“Are you certain?”\n\n“No.”\n\nHis face fell.\n\nI squeezed his fingers.\n\n“I mean I can't prove it in a laboratory. I can't tell you the exact day it started. I can't promise what we'll be in ten years.”\n\nI leaned closer.\n\n“But yes. I love you.”\n\nHis expression cracked into a small, helpless smile.\n\n“Pedantic,” he said.\n\n“You taught me.”\n\n“I regret everything.”\n\n“Liar.”\n\nHe kissed me.\n\nSlowly.\n\nThe laboratory plans crumpled under my knee.\n\nNeither of us cared.\n\nLater, we smoothed them out on the table.\n\nLily remained part of his life.\n\nSo did I.\n\nThose facts did not need to destroy each other.\n\nBoth could be true."}},"love-and-duty":{"love-and-duty-43":{"number":43,"title":"A Letter to the South","word_count":1477,"content":"The letter from the South Pole arrived folded inside a cloth packet stained with travel salt.\n\nKatara recognized the stitching before she read a word.\n\n\"My aunt made this,\" she said.\n\nHer fingers paused over the blue thread along the seam. It was the kind of pause that told me the letter mattered before I knew what it contained.\n\nAang stood by the window with Appa's feed schedule in his hand. Toph had one foot against the floor, listening to the delivery cart pull away.\n\nI stayed at the table and let Katara open it herself.\n\nShe read the first page quickly. On the second, her expression changed.\n\n\"Someone is sick?\" Aang asked.\n\n\"Not exactly.\"\n\nShe finished reading.\n\n\"The southern clinic is running low on certain herbs. The supply ships have been delayed. There are more patients than beds after the ice-breakup storms, and the healers want me to come help organize a new treatment room.\"\n\nAang put down the schedule.\n\n\"When do they need you?\"\n\n\"Now.\"\n\nToph leaned back in her chair.\n\n\"Well, that's a terrible invitation.\"\n\nKatara folded the letter along its old creases.\n\n\"It's home.\"\n\nNobody argued with that.\n\nThe harder part came later.\n\nWe had been planning to attend the Riverstone bridge inspection together, the first one after the repairs. Katara was supposed to lead the clinic volunteers at the reopened crossing. Aang had promised to speak to the council. Toph had already threatened the mortar with consequences.\n\nAnd I had planned a whole evening with Katara.\n\nI looked at the calendar we'd spent so much time making.\n\n\"How soon can you leave?\" I asked.\n\nShe looked at me with an expression I couldn't read.\n\n\"Tomorrow. Perhaps tonight if Appa is ready.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\n\"Then we need to find someone who can cover the clinic.\"\n\n\"Jasper.\"\n\nI looked up.\n\n\"You're not angry?\"\n\n\"About the letter?\"\n\n\"About leaving.\"\n\n\"Why would I be angry that people you love need you?\"\n\nShe set the letter on the table.\n\n\"Because we've barely finished deciding how to make time for each other. Because I've had to leave before. Because you have other relationships and I know you don't need me here every night.\"\n\nThe words came out in a rush. Then she looked embarrassed by how much she'd revealed.\n\nI went around the table and stood where she could see me.\n\n\"I don't need you here every night to care about you,\" I said. \"I want you to have a home you can leave and return to. I also want you to tell me when you're scared I'll stop waiting. Those are different conversations.\"\n\nToph nodded once.\n\n\"Finally, somebody said it.\"\n\nAang rubbed the back of his neck.\n\n\"I think I've been afraid of that too,\" he admitted.\n\nKatara looked at him.\n\n\"Afraid of what?\"\n\n\"That every time duty takes us in different directions, we come back a little less like we were.\"\n\nToph made a face.\n\n\"Of course we do. That's the point of living.\"\n\nWe all looked at her.\n\nShe lifted her chin.\n\n\"What? You want a relationship with people who never change? Try dating statues.\"\n\nI laughed, startled. Katara's mouth twitched.\n\nToph leaned forward.\n\n\"You'll go South. We'll miss you. You'll come back. We'll talk about what happened, and maybe something will be different. That's not a tragedy. It's just a Wednesday with a lot of snow.\"\n\nKatara crossed the room and hugged her so suddenly that Toph made an outraged noise.\n\n\"Stop making me look emotionally available!\"\n\n\"Too late,\" I said.\n\nAang joined them. I stayed back until Katara reached for me.\n\nWe spent the afternoon planning what needed planning. Aang arranged travel with Appa. I spoke to the clinic volunteers about interim shifts. Toph checked the footbridge one more time so the committee could begin its work without Katara having to carry the entire project from another continent.\n\nThe travel packet stayed on the kitchen table.\n\nI touched it once, feeling the uneven seam beneath my fingers. Katara watched me.\n\n\"What?\" she asked.\n\n\"Your aunt's stitching is crooked.\"\n\n\"She was repairing three fishing nets and a sleeping mat when she made that.\"\n\n\"Then I retract the criticism.\"\n\n\"Wise.\"\n\nShe smiled, but her eyes were wet.\n\nBefore dinner, Katara asked to speak to the clinic's coordinator through the agreed message route. She wanted to know what the staff needed, not just what the letter had frightened her into imagining.\n\nThe answer came back with a practical list. Two local healers were already managing the treatment room. They wanted Katara to demonstrate a few procedures and help revise a supply ledger, then leave them able to do the work without her. The visit was important, but she wasn't being summoned to save people who lacked their own judgment.\n\n\"That is better news than I expected,\" she said.\n\nAang offered to arrange transport but waited for her decision. Toph asked how long Appa would need to rest between the outward and return journeys. Nobody made the itinerary into a competition over who was most helpful.\n\nI looked at our calendar. \"Would you like a quiet hour with me before you go?\"\n\nKatara glanced at the departure time. \"Half an hour. And I want a little time alone to pack the medical notes.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nAang asked whether she'd like company on the first leg. She did. Toph decided to stay and check the bridge inspection rather than come along because she hated missing work she'd actually promised to do. We each had somewhere to be and someone who might miss us.\n\nI was still frightened that the house would feel different when Katara came back. Our relationship was openly polyamorous by choice, and we'd made that agreement because nobody was meant to be trapped in one version of the household. Knowing it didn't remove the ache. It just kept me from treating her trip as disloyalty.\n\nKatara took my hand for a moment. \"You don't have to be cheerful about this.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Good. I wasn't planning to be.\"\n\nThen she went to pack the notes, and we kept our small piece of time before the evening's departure.\n\nWe ate dinner early because Aang wanted to leave before the weather changed. Toph told terrible jokes about snow. Katara pretended not to enjoy them. I packed an extra scarf into her bag and tucked a small folded note beside the medical supplies.\n\nShe found it before we reached the courtyard.\n\nREAD THIS WHEN SOMEONE TELLS YOU THAT YOU HAVE TO BE EVERYWHERE AT ONCE.\n\nShe unfolded it.\n\nYOU ARE ALLOWED TO BE ONE PERSON IN ONE PLACE. WE CAN DO OUR PART HERE.\n\nKatara pressed her lips together.\n\n\"You'll make me cry before I board.\"\n\n\"Then I'll claim it's the wind.\"\n\n\"Awful excuse.\"\n\n\"Very professional.\"\n\nShe kissed me beside the open door. The air was cold, and she tasted faintly of mint tea. Her hands settled at my waist, warm through the fabric. I felt how much I wanted to ask her to stay, and how relieved I was that wanting did not have to become a demand.\n\nWhen she drew back, Aang was checking Appa's saddle.\n\nToph stood beside him, listening to the sky.\n\n\"Weather's coming,\" she said.\n\n\"Can you feel the clouds?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. But Twinkle Toes is making that face.\"\n\nAang laughed.\n\nKatara climbed onto Appa and turned to look down at us.\n\n\"I'll write.\"\n\n\"Don't promise a schedule you can't keep,\" I said.\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"I'll write when I can.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThey rose into the evening, a broad shadow moving across the rooftops. Toph stayed beside me until Appa disappeared beyond the hills.\n\n\"You're quiet,\" she said.\n\n\"Missing her already.\"\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\nShe took my hand.\n\nWe stood there until the cold reached our fingers.\n\nOver the next few days, the house changed shape without her. Aang returned after bringing Katara safely to the South, and the three of us learned where she had quietly handled small things we hadn't noticed. The clinic inventory was missing a signature. The pantry needed water. Someone had to mend the curtain that kept blowing over the stove.\n\nWe did those things.\n\nWhen a letter finally arrived, it carried two pages of Katara's handwriting and a tiny sketch of the treatment room she'd helped repair. The roof had stopped leaking. The beds were full. She was exhausted and happy to have something useful to do.\n\nAt the bottom, she had written:\n\nI MISS YOU. ALL OF YOU. PLEASE DO NOT TURN THE HOUSE INTO A DISASTER WHILE I'M GONE.\n\nToph snorted.\n\n\"Too late.\"\n\nAang looked at the pile of laundry he'd promised to fold.\n\nI folded the letter and set it on the table beside four bowls.\n\nOne of them was empty.\n\nIt did not feel like a threat.\n\nIt felt like a place waiting to be used whenever the person who belonged there chose to return."},"love-and-duty-44":{"number":44,"title":"The City from Above","word_count":1500,"content":"Appa did not approve of my map.\n\nHe made that clear by sneezing directly onto it.\n\nThe ink smudged across the east bank. I yelped, Aang laughed, and the enormous sky bison looked entirely satisfied with his contribution to urban planning.\n\n\"It's waterproof ink,\" Aang said.\n\n\"Apparently not bison-proof.\"\n\n\"Very little is.\"\n\nI held the paper away from Appa's nose. The purpose of the flight was simple: survey the Riverstone floodplain from above, compare it with the older drainage diagram, and determine whether the recent bridge repairs had changed the way stormwater traveled. We had been asked to make observations. No one had asked us to solve an entire watershed before breakfast.\n\nAang had somehow brought enough food for a journey across three continents.\n\n\"Katara packed most of it before she left,\" he admitted as he adjusted the saddle.\n\nThat stopped me.\n\n\"She packed this?\"\n\n\"She said I'd forget to eat while I was working.\"\n\n\"Accurate.\"\n\nHe smiled, but it didn't last.\n\nWe'd received one letter from the South. Katara was helping establish treatment rooms after a difficult storm season, and none of us knew when she'd be finished. The calendar we'd made before her departure was still on the kitchen wall, her planned evenings penciled in rather than crossed out.\n\nI didn't want her absence turned into an emergency. I did want to hear her voice.\n\n\"Ready?\" Aang asked.\n\nI tucked the map into its case.\n\n\"Let's go.\"\n\nAppa rose over Lanternwell Square with a slow, powerful motion that pulled my stomach toward my boots. The rooftops shrank beneath us. The seven districts took shape as actual places instead of a list of names: Tideglass Cove's bright water, Jasmine Steps curving around the garden, Brasswatch Yard with its straight workshop paths, Moonroot's dark roof, and the thin line of Starwake Pier reaching toward its own doorway.\n\nFrom above, the Lanternweald looked small enough to cup in two hands.\n\nIt was not small enough to manage carelessly.\n\n\"Look at that,\" Aang said.\n\nHe pointed toward the spring below the well. Water had gathered behind a raised strip of earth, then diverted toward the gardens. A newer footpath crossed the old streambed at an angle.\n\n\"That's not on Fen's plan,\" I said.\n\n\"Or Neri's.\"\n\nWe landed in a clear meadow above Riverstone Terrace. I drew what we'd seen while Aang paced the path and tested the soil with gentle gusts of air. His bending could clear leaves from a blocked channel. It could reveal which stones were loose. It could not make a badly placed building disappear without deciding what would happen to the people inside it.\n\n\"Could you bend the water away?\" I asked.\n\n\"Temporarily.\"\n\n\"And when you stop?\"\n\n\"It finds its old route.\"\n\nWe stood in the grass for a long time.\n\nBelow us, a small market garden had been built on what appeared to be the natural spillway. The beds were productive. Someone had planted beans, herbs, and a row of purple flowers along the edges. Moving it would cost labor, food, and perhaps someone's only income.\n\nAang crouched beside the soil.\n\n\"I used to think if I could move the water, I'd fixed the problem,\" he said.\n\n\"Sometimes you have.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I've moved it into somebody else's home.\"\n\nI marked the garden on the map.\n\n\"Then we ask the people who live there.\"\n\nWe followed the slope down to a house with blue shutters. A woman named Sela met us at the gate, wiping dirt from her hands. She had grown up tending the garden, and her grandmother had planted its first fruit tree.\n\n\"Floodwater used to run through here,\" she told us, \"but the old stones kept it low. After the new path went in, it started collecting against the west wall.\"\n\nAang looked toward the path.\n\n\"Who approved it?\"\n\n\"The neighborhood council. They needed a wider route for carts.\"\n\nI felt a familiar irritation settle behind my ribs. Every improvement carried a cost; too often the cost was paid by someone who hadn't been at the meeting.\n\n\"Did they ask you?\" I said.\n\n\"They showed us the plan after the work began.\"\n\nAang's expression changed.\n\nHe had helped design more than one public road. He knew what it felt like to be certain a plan was generous and discover that certainty had hidden an oversight.\n\n\"Would you be willing to meet with the survey group?\" he asked.\n\nSela looked at him.\n\n\"Only if they're prepared to listen.\"\n\n\"I'll make sure they are.\"\n\n\"Don't make promises for other people,\" I murmured.\n\nAang caught himself.\n\n\"I'll ask them to listen,\" he corrected. \"And I'll listen myself.\"\n\nSela nodded.\n\nWe spent the afternoon measuring the water marks along her wall. I wrote down the dates she remembered. Aang mapped the winds that carried heavy rain across the valley. We found that the new cart path had been laid on top of a narrow drainage cut, and that its packed gravel had blocked water moving toward the old service passage.\n\nSela fetched a small record book from her kitchen. She had been writing down each time rainwater reached the lowest garden bed. The entries weren't elegant, but they had dates, rough depths and the names of the crops that had been damaged.\n\n\"That one's when the new cart path was finished,\" she said. \"The water started coming through the wrong corner two storms later.\"\n\nAang studied the pages. \"Would you be willing to bring these to the survey committee?\"\n\n\"If they'll copy them instead of taking my only book.\"\n\n\"Of course,\" he began, then caught himself. \"I'll ask them to make a copy while you're there.\"\n\nI added her measurements to our map with a different symbol from the marks we'd made from above. Her records showed what happened on the ground. Our view from Appa had shown the shape of the channel but could not tell us what time of day the water entered her house.\n\nA young gardener from the next plot arrived to say that the suggested alternate path ran across their seed trays. Nobody had told them it was under consideration. Aang apologized, and we crossed the proposed shortcut out of the map instead of arguing it was temporary enough not to count.\n\nBy the time we left, we had three sets of evidence and fewer easy answers. Toph would have enjoyed that, I thought. She liked a problem better when somebody had stopped pretending their own view was complete.\n\n\"Do you think the committee will listen?\" Sela asked.\n\n\"I don't know,\" I admitted. \"But we'll bring your book back, and we'll tell you what they decide.\"\n\nShe looked at Aang.\n\n\"That, at least, is a promise you can make,\" he said.\n\nThe exchange changed nothing about the closed historic barrier. It did change who was allowed to speak before anyone moved more water across another person's home.\n\nThe seventh scratch beneath the well returned to my thoughts.\n\nWe were not ready to open anything. But we had enough evidence to stop treating the flood as a mysterious tantrum by the weather.\n\nBy the time Appa carried us home, the sky was pink.\n\nAang leaned beside me on the saddle, quiet.\n\n\"Do you miss her?\" he asked.\n\n\"Every day.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nHe looked toward the South, though there was no way to see it from here.\n\n\"Sometimes when I'm away, I worry I'm the one being left behind.\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"By Katara?\"\n\n\"By all of us. The work keeps changing. The world keeps changing. I come home and I'm afraid the shape of our family will be different.\"\n\n\"It will be different.\"\n\nHe looked startled.\n\n\"That's supposed to comfort me?\"\n\n\"Maybe not. But we're allowed to grow. The agreements we make aren't a spell keeping us frozen. We have to keep choosing them.\"\n\nAang considered that.\n\n\"What if we choose differently someday?\"\n\n\"Then we tell the truth. We don't trap people with a promise they made before they knew what they needed.\"\n\nHe was quiet for a while.\n\nThen he leaned close, brushing his forehead against mine.\n\n\"You're impossible.\"\n\n\"Toph says that.\"\n\n\"Toph says everyone is impossible.\"\n\n\"She has a point.\"\n\nHe kissed me gently, then grinned.\n\n\"Appa is watching.\"\n\n\"Appa sneezed on my map. He forfeited the right to judge.\"\n\nWhen we landed, Toph was waiting with a bowl of dumplings and a list of questions.\n\n\"Well?\" she demanded.\n\n\"We found the old spillway.\"\n\n\"Congratulations. Did the dirt look good from up there?\"\n\n\"Very picturesque,\" I said.\n\n\"Did it tell you what happens underneath the path?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nShe grinned.\n\n\"Then tomorrow we start with the ground.\"\n\nWe spread the stained map across the table. It smelled faintly of bison and evening air. Aang wrote Sela's name beside the garden, with a note that she needed to be present before anyone proposed moving it.\n\nKatara's empty chair stood across from us.\n\nI found myself smiling.\n\nThe survey had not fixed the floodplain. But we had stopped pretending the sky gave us every answer."}},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":{"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-42":{"number":42,"title":"Letter from the Old World","word_count":1393,"content":"The envelope was made from a page torn out of an old technical manual.\n\nIt arrived at Brasswatch Yard wrapped around a small packet of electrical connectors, delivered by a trader who did not know the sender's name. The paper had been folded so precisely that I could almost hear Danse correcting someone's technique.\n\nHe recognized the handwriting before he opened it.\n\nHis face went still.\n\n\"Haylen?\"\n\nI looked at the packet, then at him.\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nHe carried it into the workshop and shut the door. I stayed outside. It was not an act of generosity; it was an effort not to confuse my curiosity with his permission.\n\nFive minutes passed.\n\nThen ten.\n\nAt last, he opened the door.\n\n\"Jasper?\"\n\nI went inside.\n\nDanse stood beside the bench with a folded note in one hand. The packet of connectors lay unopened. His shoulders were set as if he expected an inspection.\n\n\"It's from Haylen,\" he said.\n\n\"Do you want to tell me what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe handed me the paper.\n\nThe message was short. Haylen wrote that the Brotherhood had changed patrol routes near a settlement Danse once supplied. Some residents were afraid a former Paladin might try to use the settlement's old radio frequency. The letter did not ask Danse to return. It asked him to avoid the channel until the people who lived there had chosen a safer way to communicate.\n\nAt the end, in smaller writing, she'd added:\n\nI HOPE YOU ARE WELL. PLEASE DON'T ANSWER THROUGH THE OLD NET.\n\nI read it twice.\n\n\"She wants you safe,\" I said.\n\n\"She wants civilians safe.\"\n\n\"Both can be true.\"\n\nDanse did not answer.\n\nHe took the note and placed it beside the purifier ledger.\n\n\"The old net is the most reliable frequency I know.\"\n\n\"That doesn't make it the right one.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe sounded irritated.\n\n\"Danse.\"\n\n\"I know, Jasper.\"\n\nHe moved to the workbench and began sorting the connectors by size, though they were already sorted. The motion was familiar enough to make my chest ache.\n\nI waited until he stopped.\n\n\"What are you afraid of?\" I asked.\n\n\"That if I don't respond, she'll think I have forgotten her.\"\n\n\"And if you respond through the channel?\"\n\n\"People could be endangered.\"\n\n\"Then the answer isn't a radio message.\"\n\nHe looked at me, the conflict plain.\n\n\"She's one of the few people who knew me before everything changed.\"\n\nI stood beside him, close enough that our sleeves touched.\n\n\"You don't have to stop caring about her to respect the boundary she's giving you.\"\n\nHis breath caught.\n\n\"That's not what I meant.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe looked down at his hands.\n\n\"I was trained to answer a call. Silence meant failure. Sometimes abandonment.\"\n\n\"Then maybe you need a different way to show you're still here.\"\n\nHe thought about it.\n\nThe following morning, we went to Neri with the note. Not to ask permission to answer Haylen, but to ask whether the Threadway courier network could deliver a sealed letter without identifying the settlement or exposing the person who had carried the first message.\n\nNeri listened and shook their head.\n\n\"Not without the recipient agreeing to receive it.\"\n\nDanse's jaw tightened.\n\n\"Then how do people communicate across the doors?\"\n\n\"They arrange it,\" Neri said. \"Or they wait.\"\n\n\"Waiting is unreliable.\"\n\n\"Sometimes it is the only honest answer.\"\n\nHe looked as though he wanted to argue. Instead, he asked for a blank page.\n\nWe returned to Brasswatch.\n\nHe wrote the letter slowly. I did not read over his shoulder. When he finished, he folded it and placed it in a plain envelope with no identifying marks on the outside.\n\nHe told me what he'd written only after he sealed it.\n\nHe had thanked Haylen for the warning. He had said he would not use the old network. He had told her that his life was different, and that the people who once served beside him still mattered. He had not asked her to put herself at risk for a reply.\n\n\"And now?\" I asked.\n\n\"Now I wait until there's a safe invitation.\"\n\nHe looked almost ill from the effort.\n\nI found two cups and poured water from the safe hand pump.\n\n\"That sounds difficult.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"Welcome to the club.\"\n\nHe took the cup.\n\n\"You make light of everything.\"\n\n\"Not everything. Just the parts that might crush us if we never laugh.\"\n\nA small smile reached his mouth.\n\nBefore we returned to the purifier, Renn found Danse studying the sealed letter in his desk drawer.\n\n\"Are we getting a new parts supplier?\" they asked.\n\n\"Not yet,\" he said.\n\nRenn held up the connector packet Haylen had sent. \"These might fit the old regulator. I wasn't going to open them without asking.\"\n\nDanse thanked them. They compared the connectors with the workshop's inventory, noting which ones could be safely tested and which would need a separate circuit check. He didn't use the parts merely because they had come from someone he trusted. A good source could still send the wrong component.\n\n\"Should we credit the sender in the public repairs ledger?\" Renn asked.\n\nDanse's hand paused.\n\n\"No personal name,\" he said. \"Record the specification and the inspection result. If the supplier ever wants to be publicly identified, that decision should be theirs.\"\n\nRenn nodded and wrote *private donation, pending test* instead. I saw a little relief move through Danse's shoulders.\n\nHe wasn't closing off Haylen's part in his life. He was keeping a correspondence safe until she could choose another way to receive it.\n\n\"You can miss somebody without transmitting on an unsafe channel,\" I said when Renn left.\n\n\"I am discovering that silence requires substantially more discipline than I was taught.\"\n\n\"Well, there's something they don't put on the recruitment poster.\"\n\nHe gave me a long look, then returned to the actual repair instructions. The connector remained on the bench until it could be tested. The letter stayed sealed.\n\nWe worked the rest of the day on the purifier's intake pipe. Severus had warned us about residues that appeared after the system sat idle. Danse had taken the criticism seriously enough to redesign the testing schedule. Every sample would be collected and labeled before anyone declared the water safe.\n\nAt noon, Tamsin stopped by with a list of residents who needed deliveries until the repairs were complete. Danse read the list carefully.\n\n\"Three households require assistance carrying water,\" he said.\n\n\"Four,\" I corrected. \"One person doesn't want their name announced publicly.\"\n\nHe looked up.\n\n\"Then we can arrange a route without displaying it.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"You're learning.\"\n\n\"I was already capable of discretion.\"\n\n\"Understatement of the century.\"\n\nHe shook his head, but there was warmth in it.\n\nThe letter stayed in a locked drawer. Not because he had stopped wanting to send it, but because wanting didn't give him the right to use a channel that might harm someone else.\n\nThat evening he found me on the workshop steps.\n\n\"Could I ask you something?\" he said.\n\n\"Always.\"\n\nHe sat beside me, the old scarf loose around his neck.\n\n\"Do you think caring about someone far away makes what I have with you less important?\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"No. You know what I believe about loving more than one person. But even outside romance, care isn't a resource we use up by remembering people.\"\n\nHe nodded slowly.\n\n\"I still have trouble believing that.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps I should say something I do believe.\" He looked directly at me. \"I want you here tonight. Not because Haylen isn't here. Because you are.\"\n\nWarmth settled behind my ribs.\n\n\"That I can do.\"\n\nHe touched my cheek with one hand, waiting for the small answering movement that told him I wanted the kiss. I leaned into his palm.\n\nHis mouth was warm. His beard scratched lightly against my skin. He was still learning how to be gentle without making gentleness feel like a protocol.\n\nWhen we parted, he kept his forehead against mine for a moment.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he said.\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For not asking me to forget.\"\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\nThe next morning, the letter was still in the drawer.\n\nThe purifier was still offline.\n\nBoth were unfinished.\n\nFor the first time, Danse seemed willing to let unfinished mean that he could keep caring without taking the wrong action simply to make the waiting stop."},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-43":{"number":43,"title":"A Refugee at the Yard","word_count":1394,"content":"The stranger at the workshop door wore an old Commonwealth coat with the insignia torn from the shoulder.\n\nThat was the first thing Danse noticed.\n\nThe second was the way they kept one hand near their satchel, as though someone had taught them to expect their belongings to be searched.\n\n\"Are you taking repair work?\" the stranger asked.\n\nDanse glanced at the bent metal housing they were carrying.\n\n\"Depending on the damage.\"\n\nThey set it carefully on the counter.\n\nIt was a pump regulator, patched twice, with a burn mark along one side.\n\n\"Can you fix it?\"\n\n\"I can assess it.\"\n\nThe stranger looked over Danse's shoulder at me.\n\n\"Does he run this place?\"\n\nDanse went very still.\n\nI understood the question before he did.\n\n\"We share the work,\" I answered. \"And we don't ask people to join a faction before we'll look at their equipment.\"\n\nThey exhaled.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nTheir name was Ash. They had come through the Brasswatch Door with permission from a local courier, following a rumor that the workshop repaired things without demanding military loyalty. They wanted a regulator fixed, temporary shelter, and a job that did not require them to tell strangers what they were.\n\nDanse examined the housing.\n\n\"This part isn't the original.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Where did you get the replacement?\"\n\n\"A machine shop near Bunker Hill.\"\n\n\"That's a dangerous route.\"\n\nAsh's jaw tightened.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe looked at their face again and seemed to hear how his question had landed.\n\n\"Can I open the casing?\" he asked.\n\nAsh nodded.\n\nThat was a better question.\n\nWe took the regulator apart on a clean mat. The internal coil had been repaired with copper wire too thin for the load, and the contacts were worn almost through. Danse explained the problem without blaming the person who had made the last repair.\n\nAsh listened carefully.\n\n\"You can fix it?\"\n\n\"Yes. It will take a day.\"\n\n\"I can pay with work.\"\n\n\"What kind?\"\n\n\"Woodworking. Cleaning. Inventory. I learn quickly.\"\n\n\"Do you have references?\"\n\nAsh's expression changed.\n\nDanse saw it.\n\n\"That was a standard question,\" he said.\n\n\"In the Commonwealth, standard questions get people killed,\" Ash replied.\n\nThe room went quiet.\n\nOutside, the temporary water station creaked as someone filled a jug. Pip called a greeting to a neighbor. The world sounded entirely ordinary, which made the fear in Ash's voice harder to ignore.\n\n\"I didn't mean to interrogate you,\" Danse said.\n\nAsh looked at him.\n\n\"You sound like the Brotherhood.\"\n\nHe did not deny it.\n\n\"I was Brotherhood.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nThe two words changed the room.\n\nDanse's hands stopped moving over the regulator. For a moment, I could see him reaching for the authority he once understood. Then he put both hands flat on the table.\n\n\"What do you need from us right now?\" he asked.\n\nAsh blinked.\n\n\"A place to stay tonight. A door that locks. No one asking me for a serial number.\"\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\n\"We have a spare room behind the yard,\" I said. \"But Neri handles temporary housing. We can ask what is available, privately.\"\n\nAsh nodded.\n\n\"Will you tell anyone why I came?\"\n\n\"Only what you give us permission to tell,\" I said.\n\nThey looked at Danse.\n\nHe took a breath.\n\n\"I won't report you.\"\n\nAsh's face did not soften.\n\n\"That's a promise people make when they want something.\"\n\nDanse accepted the criticism without argument.\n\n\"Then judge me by what I do.\"\n\nBefore anyone began the afternoon work, Tamsin asked Ash to meet her at the public counter. She had heard the offer to pay in labor and wanted to make one point clear.\n\n\"We don't exchange shelter for work here,\" she said. \"The hall room is yours under its own rules. If you want a paid shift, we can talk about that separately.\"\n\nAsh stared at her. \"You're saying I don't owe you for the key?\"\n\n\"Not with your labor.\"\n\nThey looked at me, then at Danse, still waiting to find the condition nobody had mentioned.\n\nRenn brought out the posted workshop rates and a simple task list. There were two jobs Ash might qualify for: helping repair shelves under supervision and sorting incoming materials. The work had an hourly payment and could be declined. Ash chose the shelves, after asking whether the workshop would search their belongings before each shift.\n\n\"No,\" Danse said. \"We check tools for safety before work. That is different from searching a person's bag.\"\n\nAsh didn't smile, but some of the tension went out of their shoulders.\n\nTamsin gave them the contact for the civilian complaints desk, not a promise that Danse would personally resolve every disagreement. The practical distinction seemed to matter more than another speech about welcome.\n\n\"Can I start now?\" Ash asked.\n\n\"After you see the exit route and decide whether the job suits you,\" Renn replied.\n\nDanse stayed quiet through the orientation. Watching him allow someone else to set the conditions was almost stranger than hearing him speak gently. When Ash picked up the first shelf bracket, it was as a paid worker doing a job they'd chosen, not as a refugee proving themselves worthy of the room they'd already been offered.\n\nWe spent the afternoon working quietly.\n\nAsh proved good with wood. They fixed the crooked shelf that had irritated everyone for a week. Tamsin stopped by and approved the repair with a nod. Pip brought bread and water and did not ask questions.\n\nDanse kept his distance unless Ash needed an instruction. At one point, he caught himself saying \"You will\" and stopped.\n\n\"Would you be willing to check the left bracket?\" he asked instead.\n\nAsh's mouth twitched.\n\n\"Sure.\"\n\nLater, when Ash went to speak with Neri, Danse sat on the back steps with the old regulator in his hands.\n\n\"You knew,\" I said.\n\n\"Suspected.\"\n\n\"About Ash?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked at the torn insignia on the coat hanging by the workbench.\n\n\"I used to believe every synth was a threat. That there was no meaningful difference between identity and manufacture.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I know what it feels like to have someone decide what I am before I speak.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"That doesn't automatically make you safe for everyone who's been hurt by that belief.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe said it without flinching.\n\n\"I don't want Ash to forgive me,\" he continued. \"I want to make sure the repair is sound and that nobody uses this place to hunt them.\"\n\n\"That's a good place to start.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"You don't have to reassure me.\"\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\nHe gave a short, surprised laugh.\n\nThat evening, Neri arranged a private room for Ash near the square. The key belonged to Ash; nobody else would enter without asking. The housing register listed only the help Ash had consented to request.\n\nThe next day, we finished the regulator.\n\nDanse explained what he'd replaced and showed Ash how to inspect the contacts without dismantling the whole housing. Ash followed every step.\n\n\"Does it work?\" they asked.\n\n\"Under the tested load, yes. You'll need a new coil eventually.\"\n\n\"Then I'll come back if I need one.\"\n\n\"You can.\"\n\nThey looked up at him.\n\n\"Is that an invitation or an order?\"\n\nDanse paused.\n\n\"An invitation.\"\n\nAsh nodded.\n\nThey paid with two hours of shelf repairs and left through the Brasswatch Door after choosing a safe return time.\n\nNo one applauded. No one announced that a former Paladin had become a better man. A stranger had received a repair without surrendering their private history.\n\nThat was enough.\n\nWhen the workshop closed, I found Danse cleaning the tools.\n\n\"You've been quiet,\" I said.\n\n\"Thinking.\"\n\n\"Dangerous.\"\n\nHe set the wrench down.\n\n\"Do you ever wonder whether the people we've hurt would prefer we stayed away from them?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I think the best I can do is leave them alone.\"\n\n\"Sometimes that is the best thing.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"And the other times?\"\n\n\"Ask what they need. Believe the answer. Do the work.\"\n\nDanse was silent for a while.\n\nThen he reached for my hand.\n\n\"Will you stay for dinner?\"\n\n\"I'd like that.\"\n\nHe smiled, almost shy.\n\nWe went home without carrying the day's good deed like a medal. I liked him better for it.\n\nThe next morning, the repaired shelf was still straight.\n\nThere was no lesson written on it.\n\nIt simply held what people needed it to hold."}}};let count=0;for(const series of lib.series){const edits=revisions[series.series_slug]||{};for(const chapter of series.chapters){const c=edits[chapter.id];if(!c||chapter.chapter_number!==c.number)continue;chapter.title=c.title;chapter.content=c.content;chapter.word_count=c.word_count;count++;}}window.LANTERNWEALD_PASS11_REVISIONS_B=count;})();
